From a4affebf11afed9f96fbd7b215e18a2d7debc480 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 21:14:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: Dolby Vision decoded by the processor goes to the
 bridge

A graphics card that does not decode the codec of a Dolby Vision stream
(AV1 on RTX 20 and GTX 16, AMD Renoir and Cezanne, Intel before Tiger
Lake; or VAAPI turned off) left the stream to Kodi's ordinary renderer:
it played as its base layer, without Dolby Vision, and profile 10.0
(an IPT base layer, as profile 5) with the wrong colours where nothing
reconstructed it.

The pictures of single-layer Dolby Vision that the processor decodes
(profiles 5, 8 and 10, without an enhancement layer, with their own
RPU: DVBRIDGE::CpuPicturesEligible()) now go to the bridge, for native
output, for the reconstruction as HDR10 or SDR, and for the mapping of
their base layer to SDR, as a VAAPI surface does. They go from the
three planes that CLinuxRendererGLES already uploads for its own
drawing (10-bit 4:2:0 in three GL_R16 textures, the samples as the
decoder gave them), which dvbridge_gl_frame_import() takes as
DVBRIDGE_YUV420P10: no copy is added, on the processor or on the GPU.

Exact: the composer of ETSI GS CCM 001 reads every sample of the base
layer as the integer code it holds (texelFetch and rounding to the
code), so the planes give it the codes that the P010 surface of a
hardware decoder of the same stream gives (AV1 and HEVC decoding is
exact). Measured with the software bridge on the host (zink on
lavapipe): the pictures of the FEL test stream uploaded as three planes
and as P010 give the same bytes on every way that the render test
draws; see the notes of the front. The AV1 decoding itself could not be
run on the host (no AV1 decoder in its FFmpeg, no AV1 Dolby Vision
file).

What does not change: a stream of profile 7 (its enhancement layer
needs VAAPI and is not decoded with a base layer of the processor),
HDR10 measured as Dolby Vision and HDR10+ sent as it (measured on VAAPI
surfaces), a picture that a filter made or of another format, one of an
odd size, larger than 3840x2160 or rotated: those play as before. The
pictures of a video that is not Dolby Vision are drawn as Kodi draws
them. When the processor cannot keep up, FFmpeg's AV1 decoder (dav1d)
drops pictures that are not references (skip_frame): those that come
are exact, and the bridge sees fewer of them, as it sees any gap.
DVGeometry() and ManageRenderArea() move from CRendererVAAPIGLES to the
base renderer, unchanged, so that both renderers have them.

kodi.log: "DVBridge: this Dolby Vision stream (profile N, single-layer)
is decoded by the processor (...)", once a stream, and "DVBridge: Dolby
Vision pictures that the processor decoded (WxH, 10-bit 4:2:0) go to
the bridge from the three planes ..." or "... cannot go to the bridge
...: <why>" each time the renderer is configured. The player
information no longer gives "software" as the reason for such a
stream.
