+/*!
+ * \brief Hades: whether the pictures of a Dolby Vision stream that the processor decodes (VAAPI
+ *        does not: a graphics card without hardware decoding of the codec, such as AV1 on RTX 20
+ *        and GTX 16, AMD Renoir and Cezanne or Intel before Tiger Lake, or VAAPI turned off) go to
+ *        the Dolby Vision bridge, for native output and for the reconstruction as HDR10 or SDR
+ *
+ * They go from the planes that Kodi's own renderer uploads (CLinuxRendererGLES): 10-bit 4:2:0 in
+ * three 16-bit textures as the decoder gave them, which the bridge imports as DVBRIDGE_YUV420P10.
+ * The composer of ETSI GS CCM 001 reads every sample as the integer code it holds (texelFetch,
+ * rounded): the same codes as from the P010 surface of VAAPI, so the same reconstruction (measured
+ * with the software bridge: the same bytes on every way). Single-layer Dolby Vision only:
+ * profiles 5 and 8 (HEVC) and 10 (AV1), without an enhancement layer. A stream of profile 7 has
+ * one, which needs VAAPI and is not decoded with a base layer that the processor decodes: it
+ * plays as before. And only with the stream's own RPU: HDR10 measured as Dolby Vision and HDR10+
+ * sent as it are measured on VAAPI surfaces.
+ * \param profile, enhancementLayer the stream's Dolby Vision configuration (dv_profile,
+ *        el_present_flag); \param ownMetadata its RPU is its own
+ */
+constexpr bool CpuPicturesEligible(int profile, bool enhancementLayer, bool ownMetadata)
+{
+  return ownMetadata && !enhancementLayer && (profile == 5 || profile == 8 || profile == 10);
+}
